"""Cambridge IELTS 15 Test 2 reading passages."""

P1_URBAN_DANCE = """\
A

The way we travel around our cities has a huge impact on how sustainable those cities are. In developed nations, around 30% of energy consumption is used for transport, and that figure is rising. How we travel also affects our physical and mental health, our social lives, our access to work and culture, and the air we breathe. If we want travel to be healthier and less damaging to the environment, we need radical change.

B

A surprising source of ideas is the techniques used by choreographers when they design movement in dance. Could these give urban engineers new tools for city making? The idea of putting dancers into streets and squares is appealing to many people, but that is not what this article is proposing. Instead, the suggestion is that the methods choreographers use to think about movement could help engineers plan how people actually move. The urbanist Richard Sennett has argued that urban design has suffered from a ‘separation between mind and body’. Cities have been planned as if they were diagrams, rather than places that bodies have to inhabit.

C

Medieval builders worked with an intimate knowledge of materials and with the experience of making things by hand. Modern designers, by contrast, often work through media technologies. Those technologies are useful for managing technical complexity, but they have the drawback of simplifying reality. What looks tidy on a screen may not work for people walking, waiting or sitting in the street.

D

An example is the Peachtree Center in Atlanta, USA, a well-known failure of modernist urban planning. Designers relied heavily on computer-aided design and invested too much faith in it. They did not allow for the need for protective awnings in the hot sun, so the street-level cafés could not operate. Car parks, also designed on screen, felt unwelcoming once they were built. An unforeseen problem arose because the climate of the real street had been ignored.

E

Transport engineering offers another warning. Guard rails were introduced on British roads to improve the safety of pedestrians, while ensuring that the movement of traffic is not disrupted. Pedestrians are led to access points and encouraged to cross one carriageway at a time. An unintended effect is to create psychological difficulties in crossing the road, particularly for the least mobile people. Another result is that some people cross the road in a dangerous way. The guard rails separate communities and make it more difficult to introduce forms of transport that are healthy. Because these measures have had the opposite effect to the one intended, some cities are now reversing them and taking the rails away.

F

If designers thought with their bodies, as dancers do, they might understand more clearly how movement through a city affects people. Choreography may not seem an obvious choice for engineering, but dance and engineering share an aim: both try to design patterns of movement that work for the people who have to perform them.

G

The choreographer Wayne McGregor has worked with the cognitive scientist David Kirsh on how the body can be used to simulate possible outcomes before a design is fixed. Improvising with the body can produce solutions that take account of emotional as well as functional effects. That is the kind of bodily thinking urban engineers could borrow, without turning the city into a stage.
"""

P2_DE_EXTINCTION = """\
A

The passenger pigeon went extinct on 1 September 1914, when the last known bird died in a zoo in Cincinnati. Geneticist Ben Novak is one of a group of scientists working on ‘de-extinction’: using cloning technology to turn the DNA of an extinct animal into a fertilised embryo. In the case of the passenger pigeon, that embryo would be carried by a band-tailed pigeon, a close living relative. The method aims to recreate the extinct animal from the DNA of that species alone.

B

Another candidate is the thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger. Palaeontologist Michael Archer has argued that the extinction of the thylacine may have helped a facial tumour syndrome to spread among Tasmanian devils. Bringing the thylacine back, he suggests, could restore a balance and improve the health of that remaining species.

C

Geneticist Beth Shapiro takes a more cautious view of the ethics of ‘correcting’ damage to the natural world. She describes a practical approach that uses the DNA of existing species as a template to create a hybrid animal, rather than an exact copy of the vanished one. For Shapiro, the technology is interesting, but it is not a substitute for looking after the species we still have.

D

What is the actual point of this technology if the habitat has already changed? After the passenger pigeon disappeared, North American forests lost the disturbances those huge flocks once created. Ben Novak argues that a habitat has suffered following that extinction: without the birds, the forest no longer opens up in the same way, and other native species struggle. Reintroducing a pigeon-like bird could restore those disturbances and help the vegetation recover.

E

The woolly mammoth revival project, led by Professor George Church at Harvard Medical School, is the most ambitious example. Church and his team are trying to identify the genetic traits which enabled mammoths to survive in the tundra. According to Church, introducing Asian elephants to the tundra would require physical adaptations to minimise heat loss. To survive there, the animals would need mammoth-like features: thicker hair, ears of a reduced size, and more insulating fat. Repopulating the tundra with such animals could help to reduce temperatures and decrease carbon emissions.

F

Beth Shapiro sees de-extinction mainly as a form of ‘genetic rescue’. She stresses that it is important to understand why a species went extinct in the first place, and argues that what we are doing today to preserve biodiversity is not enough. If we learned those lessons, she suggests, the further disappearance of multiple species could be avoided.
"""

P3_HAVING_A_LAUGH = """\
Humans start developing a sense of humour at about six weeks of age, when babies begin to laugh and smile in response to people around them. Laughter is universal across cultures, and it exists in some animals too, including rats and chimpanzees. For psychological scientists, laughter and humour are rich resources for studying how the human mind works.

Laughter also looks like an adaptation for social communication. In the 1950s the US sound engineer Charley Douglass created ‘laugh tracks’ for television. He understood that people enjoy humour more in a group, and he wanted viewers at home to feel as if they were sitting in a crowded theatre.

A 2016 study at the University of California, Santa Cruz, played recordings of pairs of friends or strangers laughing to listeners from 24 diverse societies. The results were strikingly consistent worldwide: people guessed correctly about 60% of the time whether the laughers were friends or strangers. What makes the study particularly significant is that similar results were produced by a wide range of cultures.

A study led by Christopher Oveis at the University of California, San Diego, looked at social hierarchies. High-status individuals produced different laughs — more dominant, higher in pitch and louder — than low-status individuals, whose laughs were more submissive. In one part of the experiment, participants exchanged roles, so that people who had been in a high-status position later took a low-status one, and the other way round. Volunteers then listened to the laughs and estimated the social status of the laugher. The results suggested that high-status individuals can always be identified by their way of laughing. The researchers had predicted that high-status people would keep producing dominant laughs, and that prediction was borne out.

Researchers David Cheng and Lu Wang at the Australian National University tested the idea that humour provides a mental break and helps to replenish mental resources. In one study, randomly chosen groups of participants were shown one of three videos, each designed to generate a different kind of emotion. When they were then given a deliberately frustrating task, those who had watched the amusing video persisted longer and tried harder than the other two groups. A second study, in which participants had to perform a particularly boring task, produced similar results. Cheng and Wang argue that humour not only reduces anxiety and helps build social connections, but may also have a stimulating effect on the body and mind. In the employee-profile task, participants were not given a fixed amount of time; they could stop when they chose. Their conclusions also challenged the usual idea that people do better on a task if they stay serious throughout.
"""
